A two-variable loop over a pull sequence, inside a lambda, inside an
initializer.

The same hole as `ternary_in_lambda_initializer.txtar` in the sibling pass.
`passIndexedIter` takes `walk.go`'s enumeration and made its `expr` hook a
no-op, so a lambda *body* in a const-context expression -- which is an ordinary
statement list -- was never walked. A `sngl:seq` sequence hands out no ordinal,
so the counter this pass binds is the whole of how `for var i, v = …` works
over one; without it the loop reached the Go emitter intact and came out as
`for i, v := range slices.Values([]int{0, 1, 2})`, which does not compile --
ranging over an `iter.Seq[V]` permits one iteration variable.

`passIndexedIter` is one of the five always-on passes precisely because it
answers for every target, so the position it could not see was not a target's
problem to catch.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `for i, v := range` -- a pull sequence yields one value; the ordinal comes from the counter

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import seq "sngl:seq"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

var totals list[int] = [1].map(func(n int) {
    var acc = 0
    for var i, v = seq.count(3) {
        acc = acc + i + v + n
    }
    return acc
})

window {
    text(value="{totals.length}")
}
